\") and int(r[\"away_goal\"])>int(r[\"home_goal\"])))\nfla_d = sum(1 for r in fla if r[\"home_goal\"]==r[\"away_goal\"])\nprint(\"Flamengo 2019 from Brasileirao_Matches:\", len(fla), \"W:\", fla_w, \"D:\", fla_d, \"L:\", len(fla)-fla_w-fla_d)\nEOF","time":{"start":1788202949239,"end":1788202949242}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Key findings:\n- BR-Football-Dataset has tournaments: Serie A, Serie B, Serie C, Copa do Brasil (overlaps with the other files!)\n- Libertadores has 1 row with NA datetime/season and \"-\" goals — a final Flamengo vs Athletico (2022 final second leg likely unplayed/scheduled). Need to handle unparseable dates and \"-\" goals gracefully.\n- Brasileirao_Matches 2012-2022 (11 seasons × 380), novo 2003-2019 (6886 — some seasons were playoff format pre-2006, e.g. 2003 had 46 teams? whatever). Overlap 2012-2019 → dedupe needed for standings.\n- Copa do Brasil round values are like \"1\", maybe \"final\" for finals? Let me check round values in Brazilian_Cup_Matches.\n\nLet me check Copa do Brasil rounds quickly, then start writing code. Also check the fifa \"Value\"/\"Wage\" columns for parsing (not critical).\n\nNow let me design the package:\n\n```\nbrazilian_soccer_mcp/\n  __init__.py\n  models.py       — Match, Player, TeamStats dataclasses\n  normalize.py    — accent stripping, state suffix, canonical keys, alias table, derby table\n  loaders.py      — per-file loaders returning list[Match]/list[Player]\n  service.py      — SoccerDataService: loads once, query methods\n  server.py       — FastMCP server with tools\nrun_server.py     — entry point\ntests/\n  conftest.py     — session-scoped service fixture\n  test_normalize.py\n  test_loaders.py\n  test_bdd_match_queries.py\n  test_bdd_team_queries.py\n  test_bdd_player_queries.py\n  test_bdd_competition_queries.py\n  test_bdd_statistics.py\n  test_server.py\nrequirements.txt\nREADME.md (update)\n```\n\nTeam normalization design:\n\n```python\ndef strip_accents(s): ...\ndef _clean(s): lowercase, strip accents, remove non-alnum → spaces, remove legal words (sport club, sport, clube, atletico clube?, ec, fc, ac, aa, sc, csa? careful), collapse spaces\nUF set = 27 brazilian states\ndef split_state(name): if name endswith \"-UF\" or \" - UF\" → (bare, uf)\ncanonical_key(name, uf=None) → f\"{bare}{-uf if uf}\"\n```\n\nLegal-word removal is risky (\"Sport\" in \"Sport-PE\" would become empty!). \"Sport Recife\" is commonly just \"Sport\". If I remove the word \"sport\" from \"sport\" alone → empty. So: only remove legal words when there are other tokens remaining. Or don't remove words at all and rely on alias table + fuzzy containment matching. \n\nMatching strategy (used by resolve):\n1. Exact canonical-key match (with/without UF): \"palmeiras-sp\" or \"palmeiras\"\n2. Alias table: curated dict mapping many name variants → canonical key\n3. Fuzzy: bare-name containment (query \"gremio\" matches \"gremio-rs\"); pick candidates; if multiple distinct teams → ambiguous.\n\nFor query resolution semantics:\n- `resolve_team(query)` → (canonical_key, display_name) if unique; raise/return None if not found; if ambiguous return list of candidates so tool can report.\n\nCanonical identity: I'll keep state suffix in key when the source provides it (all match files except BR-Football (no state) and novo (has UF columns!)). BR-Football: no state — \"Sao Paulo\", \"Flamengo\", \"Corinthians\", \"Palmeiras\" etc. So key without UF. novo: has Mandante_UF → \"atletico-mg\" style. Alias table unifies: \"sao paulo\" ↔ \"sao paulo-sp\"? SP suffix only needed for disambiguation. Solution: canonical key WITHOUT state, EXCEPT when the bare name is ambiguous across teams in registry (atletico {mg, go, pr}, america {mg, rn, ...}, internacional? no. sport? no. national? etc.).\n\nSimpler robust plan: \n- Registry: map canonical_key → display. canonical_key = bare cleaned name + (\"-\" + uf if uf known AND needed). \n- When uf unknown (BR-Football), key = bare. \n- Aliases: \"atletico-mineiro\"? no. Let me handle the big ambiguity: \"atletico\" appears as atletico-mg, atletico-go, atletico-pr (and \"athletico-pr\"). \"america\" as america-mg, america-rn (and maybe america-ce?). \"internacional\"? unique. \"nacional\"? that's Uruguayan. \"botafogo\" — botafogo-rj, botafogo-sp? In these datasets: Botafogo-RJ and Botafogo-PB? Let me not guess; build registry at load time: collect all (bare, uf) pairs from all match files. Then:\n  - ambiguous_bares = bares mapping to >1 uf (e.g. atletico, america, botafogo?, vitoria? bahia?...)\n  - canonical key for a team with known uf: bare if unambiguous else bare-uf.\n  - For teams with unknown uf (BR-Football), key = bare; if bare ambiguous (e.g. \"Atletico\"?) BR-Football uses \"Atletico GO\", \"Atletico Mineiro\", \"Atletico PR\" full-ish names probably. Let me check actual BR-Football team names for atletico.\n\nThen alias table for FIFA clubs:\n  \"atletico mineiro\" → \"atletico-mg\", \"atlético paranaense\"/\"athletico paranaense\" → \"atletico-pr\" (need athletico→atletico normalization? accent-strip gives \"atletico paranaense\"; athletico has 'h' — need explicit alias), \"america fc (minas gerais)\" → \"america-mg\", \"sport club do recife\" → \"sport\", \"ceara sporting club\" → \"ceara\", \"gremio\" → \"gremio\", \"parana\" → \"parana\"...\n\nWait — but if \"atletico\" is ambiguous with ufs mg/go/pr, then canonical keys are \"atletico-mg\",\"atletico-go\",\"atletico-pr\". A bare \"atletico\" query → ambiguous → tool reports candidates. Good, that's correct behavior.\n\nFIFA club \"Atlético Mineiro\" cleaned → \"atletico mineiro\" — alias to \"atletico-mg\". \nFIFA \"Atlético Paranaense\" → \"atletico paranaense\" — alias to \"atletico-pr\".\nFIFA \"Athletico Paranaense\"? maybe both exist; alias handles.\nFIFA \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → cleaned \"america fc minas gerais\" → alias → \"america-mg\".\nFIFA \"Sport Club do Recife\" → \"sport club recife\" → alias \"sport\".\n\nDerby table (normalized pair keys, display name):\n- fla-flu: flamengo vs fluminense\n- clássico dos milhões: flamengo vs vasco\n- grenal: gremio vs internacional\n- o clássico (clássico mineiro): atletico-mg vs cruzeiro\n- majestoso: corinthians vs sao paulo\n- choque-rei: palmeiras vs sao paulo\n- clássico alvinegro? corinthians vs palmeiras (Derby Paulista)\n- ba-vi: bahia vs vitoria\n- clássico-rei? santos vs sao paulo?\nKeep a good list: also \"atletico-mg vs america-mg\"? no. Also ceara vs fortaleza (Clássico-Rei). Also sport vs nautico (Clássico dos Clássicos), sport vs santa cruz (Clássico das Multidões), nautico vs santa cruz. Remo vs paysandu (Clássico-Rei da Amazônia). Coritiba vs athletico-pr (AtléTiCo? \"Clássico Paranaense\" Atletico-PR vs Coritiba). Vasco vs Fluminense? \"Clássico dos Gigantes\"? include main ones.\n\nI'll include: Fla-Flu, Clássico dos Milhões (Fla x Vasco), Gre-Nal, Clássico dos Milhões? no that's it, Majestoso, Choque-Rei, Derby Paulista (Corinthians x Palmeiras), Clássico Mineiro, Ba-Vi, Clássico-Rei (Ceará x Fortaleza), Clássico Paranaense, Clássico dos Clássicos (Sport x Nautico), Clássico da Amizade? skip. Also Santos x Corinthians? not standard named. Good enough — 11 derbies.\n\nCompetition normalization:\n- \"Brasileirao_Matches.csv\" → competition \"Brasileirão Série A\" (display \"Brasileirão\"), source label\n- novo → \"Brasileirão Série A\" too (historical) — same competition, different source. For dedupe: competition \"brasileirao-serie-a\", source \"campeonato_brasileiro_2003_2019\".\n- Brazilian_Cup_Matches → \"Copa do Brasil\"\n- Libertadores → \"Copa Libertadores\"\n- BR-Football tournaments: \"Serie A\" → Brasileirão Série A (source br_football), \"Serie B\" → \"Brasileirão Série B\", \"Serie C\" → \"Brasileirão Série C\", \"Copa do Brasil\" → Copa do Brasil.\n\nDedupe: matches identical (same competition key, same date, same home key, away key) — keep by source priority [brasileirao_matches, brazilian_cup_matches, libertadores_matches, novo, br_football]. Only dedupe within same competition. This gives correct standings for overlapping seasons.\n\nHmm wait, actually for standings: 2012-2019 appear in both brasileirao_matches (380/season) and novo (also 380/season?). 6886 rows for 2003-2019 = 17 seasons: 2003-2005 were playoff formats (more matches), 2006-2019 380 each. Anyway dedupe by (comp, date, home, away) handles it. But do the dates match exactly between the two files? novo has \"Data\" DD/MM/YYYY; Brasileirao has datetime. If same matches, dates should match. Some might differ (data errors) — acceptable for demo; test will verify standings 2019 Flamengo is champion with 90 pts (per spec example). Let me verify quickly after building.\n\nBR-Football Serie A 2014-2023 also overlaps with brasileirao_matches 2014-2022 — dedupe by date+teams. Same for Copa do Brasil. Good.\n\nMatch record needs: date (datetime.date or None), home_display, away_display, home_key, away_key, home_goals (int|None), away_goals (int|None), competition display + key, season (int|None), round/stage (str), venue, source, plus extended stats (corners, shots, attacks, halftime scores) when available.\n\nPlayer model: id, name, age, nationality, overall, potential, club (display), club_key (resolved or None), position, jersey, height, weight, preferred foot, value, wage... keep subset.\n\nService methods:\n- `search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, limit=50)` — team matches opponent when both given (either order). Returns list[Match] + total count.\n- `head_to_head(team, opponent, competition=None)` → matches + summary {a_wins, b_wins, draws, a_goals, b_goals}.\n- `team_stats(team, season=None, competition=None, venue=None)` → per competition breakdown, overall, home/away splits.\n- `team_competitions(team)` → competitions and seasons the team played in, match counts.\n- `standings(competition=\"Brasileirão Série A\", season)` → computed table. For Copa do Brasil/Libertadores standings don't make sense (knockout) — but Libertadores has group stage; skip standings for cups, or compute \"group-stage table\"? Too complex; standings only for league competitions (Serie A/B/C). Return error message for cups. Actually spec asks \"Who won the 2019 Brasileirão?\" → standings(2019) → champion. Also \"Which teams were relegated in 2020?\" → bottom 4 of standings. Good.\n- `competition_seasons(competition)` → seasons + match counts.\n- `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, n=10)` → sorted by goal margin.\n- `league_statistics(competition=None, season=None)` → avg goals/match, home win %, draw %, away win %, total matches, biggest win.\n- `best_records(venue=\"home\"|\"away\"|\"overall\", competition=None, min_matches=...)` → ranked teams by win rate / points.\n- `derbies(season=None, competition=None)` → matches between known derby pairs + which derby.\n- `search_players(name=None, club=None, nationality=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, limit=30, sort=\"overall\")`.\n- `top_players(club=None, nationality=None, n=10)`.\n- `players_by_club(nationality=\"Brazil\")` → Brazilian clubs with counts of Brazilian players + avg rating (for the \"Brazilian players at Brazilian clubs\" answer format).\n- `list_teams(query=None, competition=None, season=None)` → team registry search.\n- `resolve_team(query)` → exact/alias/fuzzy resolution with ambiguity reporting.\n\nMCP server tools (FastMCP):\n1. search_matches\n2. head_to_head\n3. team_stats\n4. team_competitions\n5. list_teams\n6. search_players\n7. top_players\n8. brazilian_players_by_club\n9. standings\n10. competition_info\n11. derby_matches\n12. biggest_wins\n13. league_statistics\n14. best_records\n\nAll return structured JSON-serializable dicts (FastMCP converts dicts to JSON text content? Actually FastMCP structured output — returning dict works with output_schema in MCP; the SDK will handle dict returns by serializing). I'll return dicts; FastMCP (v2.x SDK) supports dict returns as structured content.\n\nTests (BDD GWT style per spec): Use pytest with classes named per feature, methods test_ with Given/When/Then comments and structure. The spec's example:\n```gherkin\nGiven the match data is loaded\nWhen I search for matches between \"Flamengo\" and \"Fluminense\"\nThen I should receive a list of matches\nAnd each match should have date, scores, and competition\n```\n\nI'll write tests that mirror these scenarios with Given/When/Then phases (maybe via pytest-bdd? Not installed; pytest-bdd would be nice but requires install — network works, but keeping deps minimal: implement GWT structure manually with helper functions given_/when_/then_ style naming, documented. Actually let me just install pytest-bdd? The task says \"test using BDD GWT structured PyTest\" in sample prompt. TASK.md says \"Use BDD (Behavior-Driven Development) test scenarios\" with gherkin examples. I can implement with pytest-bdd (gherkin .feature files + step defs) — that most literally satisfies \"BDD GWT structured PyTest\". Let me install pytest-bdd into the venv. It works with pytest 9? pytest-bdd latest supports pytest>=8. Let me try installing.\n\nIf pytest-bdd install fails, fallback to manual GWT-structured tests.\n\nAlso update README.md describing what was done. And requirements.txt. And maybe pyproject.toml. Also `run_server.py` entry.\n\nThe task says \"Write all code files to the current directory\" — so package folder inside cwd. Good.\n\nLet me also verify standings for 2019 Flamengo = 90 pts after dedupe, in tests. Note: Flamengo 2019 in real life: 90 pts? Actually real: Flamengo 2019 had 90 points? They had 28W 6D 4L? Hmm, 2019 Flamengo: 90 points? Let me think: 38 matches, 26W 8D 4L = 86? No... Flamengo 2019: 28 wins? Real answer: Flamengo 2019: 90 points (28W 6D 4L)? Hmm, I don't want to hard-code a possibly-wrong value. Spec's example says \"1. Flamengo - 90 pts (28W, 6D, 4L) - Champion\" — 28*3+6=90. The spec literally gives this as expected answer format. I'll assert champion == Flamengo and points == 90 (spec says so). But wait — the dataset (Brasileirao_Matches 2019, 380 matches) must produce that. If dedupe works, standings from 380 matches. Flamengo 2019 real record: W28 D6 L4? Actually I believe Flamengo 2019 was 28W-6D-4L = 90 pts? Hmm, that sounds high but 2019 Flamengo was legendary (Gabigol, Bruno Henrique, Jorge Jesus). Real: 90 points yes — 28 wins, 6 draws, 4 losses? That sums to 38. Yes, I recall 90 points. Good, matches spec.\n\nBut careful: dedupe between brasileirao_matches 2019 and novo 2019 AND br_football Serie A 2019. If dates differ slightly between sources, some duplicates remain → Flamengo >38 matches → wrong points. I'll verify empirically and adjust (e.g. dedupe also by (comp, season, home, away, round?) — safer: (comp, season, round, home, away) for leagues! Round+teams+season is a solid key; date errors won't break it. For cups, use (comp, season, round, home, away) too... but Copa do Brasil has two-leg ties (round \"Semifinal\" games 1 and 2 — round values?). Let me check cup round values. If rounds like \"final\", \"semifinal\" without leg numbers, date+teams needed. I'll use (comp, season, date, home, away) primarily but ALSO (comp, season, home, away, round) as fallback key for leagues... Let me check the actual overlap empirically first.\n\nLet me check cup rounds and overlap between sources now.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_059339a24001BJl4NFq5mkT6QE","sessionID":"ses_fa6cfe050ffe51wxe0DFh4LAA3","messageID":"msg_059326155001V1FyStQxiD2yB1"}}
