or r in bra if \"Flamengo\" in (r[\"home_team\"], r[\"away_team\"])]` → returned 0. Second script: `fla = [r for r in rows if r[\"season\"]==\"2019\" and \"Flamengo\" in (r[\"home_team\"], r[\"away_team\"])]` → 0. But now counting home games gives 19 for Flamengo-RJ in 2019?!\n\nWait — the second script printed \"fla 2019 count: 0\" while this third script says fla home games 19 in 2019. Contradiction! Unless... the \"in\" check: `\"Flamengo\" in (r[\"home_team\"], r[\"away_team\"])` — tuple membership uses ==, not substring! `\"Flamengo\" in (\"Flamengo-RJ\", \"Botafogo-RJ\")` is False because tuple membership checks equality with each element, not substring! That's the bug in my test scripts. Duh. So Flamengo data is fine.\n\nOK so data is fine; my quick scripts were wrong. Flamengo-RJ 19 home games in 2019. Let me redo the overlap + standings check properly with substring/equality checks.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_059343ccc0015Ow1oD7Ujh9xYZ","sessionID":"ses_fa6cfe050ffe51wxe0DFh4LAA3","messageID":"msg_05933f573001fXq3bNI4ADwhnP"}}
