{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788203261793,"sessionID":"ses_fa6cfe050ffe51wxe0DFh4LAA3","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_b98f2c111b5149c5a89c1c57","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"./venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import Counter\nwith open(\"data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    cup = list(csv.DictReader(f))\nper = Counter((r[\"season\"], r[\"round\"]) for r in cup)\nseasons = sorted({r[\"season\"] for r in cup})\nfor s in seasons:\n    rounds = {rnd: per[(s,rnd)] for rnd in sorted({r[\"round\"] for r in cup if r[\"season\"]==s})}\n    print(s, rounds)\nEOF"},"output":"2012 {'1': 53, '2': 29, '3': 16, '4': 8, '5': 4, '6': 2}\n2013 {'1': 2, '2': 69, '3': 37, '4': 20, '5': 16, '6': 8, '7': 4, '8': 2}\n2014 {'1': 2, '2': 71, '3': 37, '4': 20, '5': 15, '6': 8, '7': 4, '8': 2}\n2015 {'1': 2, '2': 70, '3': 36, '4': 20, '5': 16, '6': 8, '7': 4, '8': 2}\n2016 {'1': 75, '2': 37, '3': 20, '4': 16, '5': 8, '6': 4, '7': 2}\n2017 {'1': 40, '2': 20, '3': 20, '4': 10, '5': 16, '6': 8, '7': 4, '8': 2}\n2018 {'1': 40, '2': 20, '3': 20, '4': 10, '5': 16, '6': 8, '7': 4, '8': 2}\n2019 {'1': 40, '2': 20, '3': 20, '4': 10, '5': 16, '6': 8, '7': 4, '8': 2}\n2020 {'1': 40, '2': 20, '3': 20, '4': 10, '5': 16, '6': 8, '7': 4, '8': 2}\n2021 {'1': 40, '2': 20, '3': 32, '4': 16}\n","metadata":{"output":"2012 {'1': 53, '2': 29, '3': 16, '4': 8, '5': 4, '6': 2}\n2013 {'1': 2, '2': 69, '3': 37, '4': 20, '5': 16, '6': 8, '7': 4, '8': 2}\n2014 {'1': 2, '2': 71, '3': 37, '4': 20, '5': 15, '6': 8, '7': 4, '8': 2}\n2015 {'1': 2, '2': 70, '3': 36, '4': 20, '5': 16, '6': 8, '7': 4, '8': 2}\n2016 {'1': 75, '2': 37, '3': 20, '4': 16, '5': 8, '6': 4, '7': 2}\n2017 {'1': 40, '2': 20, '3': 20, '4': 10, '5': 16, '6': 8, '7': 4, '8': 2}\n2018 {'1': 40, '2': 20, '3': 20, '4': 10, '5': 16, '6': 8, '7': 4, '8': 2}\n2019 {'1': 40, '2': 20, '3': 20, '4': 10, '5': 16, '6': 8, '7': 4, '8': 2}\n2020 {'1': 40, '2': 20, '3': 20, '4': 10, '5': 16, '6': 8, '7': 4, '8': 2}\n2021 {'1': 40, '2': 20, '3': 32, '4': 16}\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"./venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import Counter\nwith open(\"data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    cup = list(csv.DictReader(f))\nper = Counter((r[\"season\"], r[\"round\"]) for r in cup)\nseasons = sorted({r[\"season\"] for r in cup})\nfor s in seasons:\n    rounds = {rnd: per[(s,rnd)] for rnd in sorted({r[\"round\"] for r in cup if r[\"season\"]==s})}\n    print(s, rounds)\nEOF","time":{"start":1788203261789,"end":1788203261792}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"brf team sets are the same clubs — the only difference is my canon() gives different keys because brf has no UF (e.g. \"bahia\" vs \"bahia-ba\"). So the UF-suffix from bra creates suffixed keys while brf creates bare keys — they don't merge without the registry logic. This confirms the design: I need the registry-based canonicalization where \"bahia\" (bare, no uf) merges into \"bahia-ba\" when bare is ambiguous, or where unambiguous bares keep the bare key.\n\nWait, actually — think again about what canonical keys should BE:\n\nOption A: key = bare-uf whenever uf is KNOWN for that team (bahia-ba, flamengo-rj, palmeiras-sp, corinthians-sp...). Then brf's bare names need registry lookup to find their uf → merge.\n\nOption B: key = bare when unambiguous, bare-uf when ambiguous. With registry-driven merge for None-uf entries.\n\nThe team sets here show bra 2019 vs brf 2019 differ only by suffix — so with proper merging, both sources cover the same 20 clubs per season. brf 2020 has only 264 Serie A matches (incomplete); brf 2021 has 491 with 24 teams (includes Serie B games mislabeled? or data issues); brf 2023 has 377.\n\nSo merging is possible but standings MUST be computed per (comp, season) from a single best source, or with round-key dedupe:\n- bra (2012-2022): 380/season complete ✓\n- novo (2003-2019): complete per season ✓\n- brf Serie A (2014-2023): 264-491/season, messy.\n\nFor standings priority: brasileirao_matches > novo > brf. For seasons only in brf (2023) use brf. But brf 2023 has 377 matches with 20 teams — probably near-complete Serie A 2023 (real 2023 had 380). Good enough; test only 2019.\n\nDedupe strategy for the global match pool (used by search/head-to-head/team stats):\n- Dedupe key: (comp_key, season, round, home_key, away_key) for sources WITH round (bra, novo, cup); (comp_key, season, stage, home_key, away_key) for libertadores; brf has NO round and NO stage — dedupe brf against others by (comp_key, season, date, home_key, away_key)?\n- brf vs bra date overlap is only 96 matches — so brf league matches will mostly REMAIN as near-duplicates with different dates in the search pool. E.g. searching \"Palmeiras 2019\" would show the same fixture twice (once from bra with date X, once from brf with date Y). Confusing but... how different are the dates actually? Let me check a specific fixture: Internacional x Atletico-MG 2019: brf says 2019-12-08. What does bra say? If bra dates are systematically different (e.g. bra dates are WRONG/shifted), maybe one source is more trustworthy.\n\nHmm, let me check a few known fixtures. Flamengo won the 2019 title on Nov 24, 2019? The clinching match was Flamengo 1-0? Actually Flamengo clinched with a win over... hmm, November 2019. Let me look at bra's dates for the last round of 2019 and brf's.\n\nActually, let me step back and think about what matters for the benchmark:\n\n1. Search results should not show the same fixture 2-3 times with different dates — at least for the main competitions.\n2. Standings must be correct (2019: Flamengo 90).\n3. Head-to-head records should be sane (Fla-Flu counts).\n\nPragmatic solution — per-(competition, season) SOURCE PRIORITY for ALL queries:\n- For \"Brasileirão Série A\" + season in 2012-2022 → only use bra matches; 2003-2011 → only novo; 2023 → only brf. \n- Copa do Brasil: 2012-2021 → Brazilian_Cup_Matches; 2022-2023 → brf.\n- Serie B/C → brf only.\n- Libertadores → its file only.\n\nThis \"source resolution\" per (comp, season) eliminates ALL duplicates deterministically without fuzzy dedupe. If season not specified in a query (e.g. \"all Fla-Flu matches\"), pool = union over seasons with per-season source resolution → each fixture appears exactly once! This is clean, deterministic, and testable. \n\nImplementation: `source_for(comp_key, season)` → set of source ids; match pool filtered accordingly. Rules:\n- comp \"brasileirao-serie-a\": seasons 2012-2022 → {\"brasileirao_matches\"}; 2003-2011 → {\"campeonato_2003_2019\"}; 2023 → {\"br_football\"}; other seasons → all sources (fallback).\n- comp \"copa-do-brasil\": 2012-2021 → {\"brazilian_cup_matches\"}; else {\"br_football\"}.\n- comp \"libertadores\": {\"libertadores_matches\"}.\n- comp \"serie-b\"/\"serie-c\": {\"br_football\"}.\n\nHmm wait — is novo 2003-2011 complete? 2003-2005 were weird formats (46 teams, 2003 had 552 matches? whatever). novo per-season completeness doesn't matter; it's the only source for those seasons.\n\nBut hmm, one more consideration: brf Serie A 2014-2022 will be EXCLUDED entirely (bra covers those). That's fine — brf adds Serie B, Serie C, and 2023 Serie A + extra cup years. The spec says \"All 6 CSV files are loadable and queryable\" ✓ (all loaded; brf queryable via Serie B/C/2023 + its extra stats columns appear when those matches are returned... wait, the extended stats (corners/shots/attacks) only exist in brf rows. The spec mentions \"Extended Match Statistics\" as a dataset — corner stats queries. I'll expose match stats in search results when present, and allow source override param `include_all_sources=True` for stats-specific queries. Let me add a `search_matches(..., source=None)` param where source can force \"br_football\" to get stat-rich rows. Also league_statistics can report corners etc. when using brf source.)\n\nHmm, hold on, let me reconsider. Actually let me check whether brf Copa do Brasil 2012-2021 duplicates Brazilian_Cup_Matches, and brf 2022-2023 cup. brf tournaments Copa do Brasil = 1123 matches across 2014-2023. Brazilian_Cup_Matches = 1337 across 2012-2021. For seasons 2014-2021 both have data — the source-priority rule handles it (cup file wins 2012-2021, brf wins 2022-2023).\n\nOK this design is solid. Now, one more check: novo 2012-2019 vs bra 2012-2019 — with source priority, novo is EXCLUDED for those seasons entirely. novo is the source for 2003-2011. Its Arena column is nice extra info for old matches.\n\nWait, actually — what about queries that hit ALL competitions for a team across all seasons, e.g. team_stats(\"Palmeiras\")? Per-season source resolution applies per competition: brasileirão 2003-2011 from novo, 2012-2022 from bra, 2023 from brf; cup from cup file (2012-2021) + brf (2022-23); libertadores from lib. No duplicates ✓.\n\nEdge: team_stats(\"Palmeiras\") aggregates ~ (19 novo seasons + 11 bra seasons) + cup + libertadores... fine.\n\nWhat about a team that ONLY exists in brf Serie B (e.g. \"Vila Nova\")? list_teams / team_stats find it via serie-b matches ✓.\n\nNOW, one important question: does brf have Serie A 2023 (the only 2023 Serie A source)? Yes, 377 matches. And Copa do Brasil 2022/2023 from brf. Good.\n\nAlso Libertadores has that one NA row (Flamengo vs Athletico final, unplayed — it's the 2022 final 2nd leg? Flamengo won 2022 Libertadores beating Athletico-PR in the final... the final was played Nov 2022 single match at Guayaquil? Actually 2022 final: Flamengo 1-0 Athletico. The dataset row has no score/datetime — likely a placeholder. Handle: goals None → skip in stats, include in schedule-less? I'll just drop unparseable rows? NO — keep with date=None, goals=None, and exclude from standings/stats computations. Searchable as \"scheduled/unknown\". Simpler: keep row, mark has_result=False.)\n\nCup file round numbers 1-8: round 8 = final (14 matches over 10 seasons? \"8\": 14 — hmm finals should be ~10 per 2012-2021... 14 is fine, some finals two-legged). \"Find all Copa do Brasil finals\" → filter stage/round == 8 → I'll map cup rounds to names: 1..3 \"Round N\"/first rounds, but actual names: Round 1, Round 2, Round 3, Round 4 (round of 16), Round 5 (round of 8? no...). Copa do Brasil structure: First round, Second round, Third round, Round of 16 (4th), Quarterfinals (5th? no 6th)... hmm 2012-2021 format: Round 1 (80 teams), Round 2, Round 3, Round of 16, Quarterfinals, Semifinals, Final — that's 7. The file has 8 rounds with counts 334,376,258,140,123,62,30,14 — roughly halving each round: r1 334? (167+167 ties?)... doesn't matter — I'll label rounds generically: \"Round 8 (final)\"? Risky to name wrongly. I'll expose round as \"Round 8\" and map round 8 → \"Final\" only if count small (14/10 seasons ≈ finals-ish). Hmm 2012-2021 = 10 seasons; finals: 2012-2019 two-legged (2 matches each) + 2020-2021 single = 18ish. 14 close enough. But actually with counts r7=30 (semifinals ~2 per season×2 legs=40?) whatever. Let me check: cup round 8 by season → if 1-2 per season → final. Quick check later. I'll implement CUP_ROUND_NAMES = {1:\"First round\",2:\"Second round\",3:\"Third round\",4:\"Round of 16\",5:\"Round of 32\"?...} — no, let me check the data shape first: for season 2012, rounds 1-8 counts. If r4=32ish→round of 32... Let me check in the next script. Actually the 2012+ format: R1: 80 teams → 40 matches... over 2012-2021, r1 should be ~40×5 + ... The counts (334, 376, 258, 140, 123, 62, 30, 14) look like: r1 334 (early rounds with many small clubs), r2 376?? more than r1 — weird. Whatever — I'll label them \"Round N\" and treat round 8 as \"Final\" ONLY after verifying per-season counts are 1-2. If not, provide stage filter that accepts \"final\" → round 8. Let me verify.\n\nThen FIFA data: club → canonical team key mapping for Brazilian clubs (so \"players at Flamengo\" cross-references match data team \"flamengo-rj\"). FIFA club names for Brazilian clubs need aliases to my canonical keys. FIFA names seen: 'Grêmio', 'Atlético Mineiro', 'Cruzeiro', 'Fluminense', 'Santos', 'Internacional', 'América FC (Minas Gerais)', 'Botafogo', 'Bahia', 'Paraná', 'Atlético Paranaense', 'Vitória', 'Sport Club do Recife', 'Chapecoense', 'Ceará Sporting Club'. Full list of Brazilian clubs in FIFA data — let me extract those and map. My canonical keys (registry-based): gremio? \"gremio\" bare unambiguous? registry \"gremio\" ufs {RS} only → key \"gremio\". flamengo ambiguous {RJ, PI, None} → key \"flamengo-rj\". palmeiras {SP} → \"palmeiras\". corinthians {SP} → \"corinthians\". sao paulo {SP} → \"sao paulo\". santos {None, AP, SP} → ambiguous → \"santos-sp\". vasco da gama → \"vasco da gama\"? registry \"vasco da gama\" ufs {RJ} → \"vasco da gama\". fluminense {RJ} → \"fluminense\". botafogo {None, PB, RJ} → \"botafogo-rj\". bahia {BA, BH} → \"bahia-ba\". vitoria {BA, ES} → \"vitoria-ba\". atletico {ES,MG,GO,BA,AC,PR} → \"atletico-mg\"/\"atletico-go\"/\"atletico-pr\". america {RN, MG} → \"america-mg\"/\"america-rn\". sport {PE} → \"sport\". ceara {CE} → \"ceara\". fortaleza {CE} → \"fortaleza\". chapecoense {SC} → \"chapecoense\". coritiba {PR} → \"coritiba\". cruzeiro {MG} → \"cruzeiro\". internacional {None, RS, SC} → \"internacional-rs\"? Hmm — \"internacional\" ambiguous {None,RS,SC}: Internacional-SC (a small Santa Catarina club \"Internacional-SC\"?? in cup file). Frequency → internacional-rs major. Key \"internacional-rs\"? But then brf \"Internacional RS\" → uf RS → \"internacional-rs\" ✓, brf \"Internacional\" → bare no uf → frequency merge → \"internacional-rs\" ✓.\n\nSo the algorithm at load time:\n1. Parse all (display_name, uf_hint) from all match files.\n2. Compute bare → {uf: match_count}.\n3. If bare has multiple ufs → each uf gets key bare-uf; None-uf matches merge into the uf with the highest count (major club).\n4. If bare has exactly one uf → key = bare (regardless), all None-uf matches merge in.\n   Wait but \"atletico\" with ufs {ES,MG,GO,BA,AC,PR}: keys atletico-es, atletico-mg, atletico-go, atletico-ba, atletico-ac, atletico-pr — but \"Atletico-BA\"?? Atletico de Alagoinhas (cup file). OK.\n   Hmm — but what about display \"Athletico\" (libertadores \"Athletico\" bare, no uf, 2019-2022 rows)? bare \"athletico\" ≠ \"atletico\" — needs alias \"athletico\"→\"atletico-pr\". Add to alias table (Athletico PR renamed from Atletico PR in 2019; in these files \"Athletico\" alone = Athletico-PR). Also novo has \"Athletico-PR\" probably → parse → bare \"athletico\" uf PR → alias \"athletico-pr\"→\"atletico-pr\"? I'll add explicit aliases: \"athletico-pr\"→\"atletico-pr\", \"athletico\"→\"atletico-pr\".\n\n5. Curated ALIAS table applied BEFORE uf-key logic (maps full names to explicit keys): \"atletico mineiro\"→\"atletico-mg\", \"atletico goianiense\"→\"atletico-go\", \"atletico paranaense\"/\"athletico paranaense\"→\"atletico-pr\", \"sport recife\"/\"sport club recife\"→\"sport\", \"cs alagoano\"→\"csa\", \"america mineiro\"/\"america fc minas gerais\"→\"america-mg\", \"ponte pretta\"→\"ponte preta\", \"red bull brasil\"? distinct from bragantino. \"bragantino\" bare unambiguous? registry {PA, SP} → ambiguous! \"Bragantino-PA\"?? weird (a small Pará club in the cup file? \"Bragantino-PA\" exists in brf too). Keys: \"bragantino-sp\" (major, Red Bull) + \"bragantino-pa\". Query \"Bragantino\" → frequency → bragantino-sp ✓.\n\nFIFA clubs alias → canonical keys:\n- \"gremio\"→\"gremio\", \"atletico mineiro\"→\"atletico-mg\", \"cruzeiro\"→\"cruzeiro\", \"fluminense\"→\"fluminense\", \"santos\"→\"santos-sp\", \"internacional\"→\"internacional-rs\", \"america fc minas gerais\"→\"america-mg\", \"botafogo\"→\"botafogo-rj\", \"bahia\"→\"bahia-ba\", \"parana\"→\"parana\", \"atletico paranaense\"→\"atletico-pr\", \"vitoria\"→\"vitoria-ba\", \"sport club do recife\"→\"sport\", \"chapecoense\"→\"chapecoense\", \"ceara sporting club\"→\"ceara\", \"flamengo\"→\"flamengo-rj\", \"palmeiras\"→\"palmeiras\", \"corinthians\"→\"corinthians\", \"sao paulo\"→\"sao paulo\", \"vasco da gama\"→\"vasco da gama\", \"sport\"... plus others (goias, fortaleza, coritiba, avai, csa, ceara...).\n\nThe generic resolver handles most: canon_fifa(\"Santos\") → bare \"santos\" no uf → frequency merge → \"santos-sp\" ✓. So FIFA clubs can reuse the same resolve machinery! Nice — no special FIFA table needed except where FIFA names differ from bare names (\"atletico mineiro\", \"america fc minas gerais\", \"ceara sporting club\" — noise removal handles: \"ceara sporting club\" → tokens [ceara, sporting, club] remove \"club\" → \"ceara sporting\" — NOT \"ceara\"! \"sporting\" is not in my NOISE set. Add \"sporting\"? For \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → yes add \"sporting\"? But \"Sporting\" could be a club name elsewhere (Sporting Cristal in Libertadores! \"Sporting Cristal\" → removing \"sporting\" → \"cristal\" WRONG). Hmm. Add explicit alias \"ceara sporting\"→\"ceara\" instead. And \"atletico mineiro\" alias, \"america fc minas gerais\"→\"america mg\"→ uf logic → \"america-mg\" ✓ (since tokens end with \"gerais\"? no — \"(Minas Gerais)\" parenthetical removed → \"america fc\" → bare \"america\" no uf → frequency merge → america-mg? \"america\" frequency: MG vs RN — MG way more → \"america-mg\" ✓ lucky. But is it right? América-RN had many seasons in Serie B... América-MG count in match files vs América-RN: both appear; MG probably more. Risky but frequency is a reasonable heuristic; ambiguity reporting covers the rest.)\n\nHmm wait, actually there's a subtlety: for FIFA \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" the parenthetical has the state! Better: extract UFs from parentheticals too: \"America FC (Minas Gerais)\" → look up \"minas gerais\"→MG state name map? I'll add a small map of state names→UF for parentheticals: (Minas Gerais)→MG, (Rio de Janeiro)→RJ, (Sao Paulo)→SP? Hmm, only \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" needs it. Alias table: \"america fc minas gerais\" is moot since parenthetical removed pre-tokenizing... Let me just special-case: if parenthetical contains a state NAME → use as uf hint. Simple map: {\"minas gerais\":\"MG\",\"rio de janeiro\":\"RJ\",\"sao paulo\":\"SP\",\"rio grande do sul\":\"RS\",\"parana\":\"PR\",...}. OK fine.\n\nRight — enough design. Time to write the actual code. Architecture:\n\n```\nbrazilian_soccer_mcp/\n  __init__.py        — version\n  normalize.py       — name canonicalization, registry building, date parsing\n  models.py          — Match, Player, TeamRecord dataclasses\n  loaders.py         — load all 6 CSVs → matches, players\n  service.py         — SoccerDataService with query methods\n  server.py          — FastMCP server + tool functions\ntests/\n  conftest.py        — session fixtures (service, matches, players)\n  test_normalize.py  — name/date normalization unit tests\n  test_loaders.py    — all 6 files load, counts, models\n  test_bdd_match_queries.py      — GWT BDD\n  test_bdd_team_queries.py\n  test_bdd_player_queries.py\n  test_bdd_competition_queries.py\n  test_bdd_statistics.py\n  test_mcp_server.py — in-process MCP client session test\nrun_server.py\nrequirements.txt\nREADME.md (update)\n```\n\nBDD style: The TASK says \"Use BDD (Behavior-Driven Development) test scenarios\" with Gherkin. I'll install pytest-bdd and write real .feature files + step definitions — that most literally fulfills it. Let me check pytest-bdd compatibility with pytest 9. pytest-bdd 8.x requires pytest>=8.1 — probably works with 9? Let me try installing; if it breaks, fall back to manual GWT.\n\nLet me now also decide the source-resolution table precisely, and verify: cup file seasons 2012-2021 vs brf Copa do Brasil seasons 2014-2023. And check novo seasons 2003-2011 only in novo? bra starts 2012. novo 2012-2019 excluded via priority. And what about novo 2003-2011 round robin? Fine.\n\nWait — one more important check: novo contains 2012-2019 with DIFFERENT round numbering partially (only ~300/380 round-key overlap). With source priority, novo is fully excluded for 2012+ — no issue.\n\nAlso Libertadores 2013-2022 (\"NA\" season 1 row — season None). cup rounds — check cup round 8 per season now. And check what \"NA\" datetime rows look like elsewhere. Also FIFA \"Value\"/\"Wage\" columns format (€110.5M strings) — skip parsing, keep raw.\n\nNow write the code. First install pytest-bdd.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_059385f18001dFguMOAxx8YCC3","sessionID":"ses_fa6cfe050ffe51wxe0DFh4LAA3","messageID":"msg_059370195001vyH7IyTAv5xqeb"}}
