eiras\" {SP} → \"palmeiras\" ✓. \"sao paulo\" {SP} → \"sao paulo\" ✓. \"sport\" {PE} → \"sport\" ✓. \"ceara\" {CE} → \"ceara\" ✓. \"fortaleza\" {CE} → \"fortaleza\" ✓. \"coritiba\" {PR} → \"coritiba\" ✓. \"atletico-pr\" ✓ via bare \"atletico\" multi-uf. \"botafogo\" {None, PB, RJ} → botafogo-rj, botafogo-pb ✓.\n\nresolve(query) algorithm:\n1. parse query → bare, uf, country (also parenthetical state-name → uf).\n2. alias check: alias.get(f\"{bare}-{uf}\") or alias.get(bare) → if value in keys → resolved.\n3. if country: key = f\"{bare}-{country.lower()}\" → if in keys → resolved; else bare-key.\n4. if uf: \n   - k = f\"{bare}-{uf}\" if in keys → resolved\n   - elif bare in keys (and bare's registry uf set == {uf} implicitly since bare-key means single uf) → resolved (uf agrees)\n   - elif bare in multi-uf bares → uf contradicts → try bare?? NO: report not found + suggestions. Hmm — \"Vitoria-ES\" query → vitoria-es IS a key (small club, real) → resolved ✓. \"Atletico-BA\" → atletico-ba key exists ✓. Contradiction case rare.\n   - else: fall through to no-uf logic ignoring the uf? e.g. \"Flamengo-SP\"?? → flamengo-sp not a key → suggestions for flamengo-*. I'll fall through to no-uf logic (freq pick among bare variants) with alternatives noted.\n5. no uf: bare in keys → resolved. Else if bare is multi-uf bare → candidates = [f\"{bare}-{uf}\"...], pick by total match count desc → resolved with alternatives. Else fuzzy: keys whose bare-token-set ⊆ query-token-set (or query tokens ⊆ key tokens?) — implement subset both ways? Let me do: for key in keys: key_tokens ⊆ query_tokens (key more general) → match. Also query_tokens ⊆ key_tokens?? \"paulista\" → ? skip. Single-direction: key tokens ⊆ query tokens. \"sport club corinthians paulista\" → tokens {sport, club, corinthians, paulista} minus noise {club} → {sport, corinthians, paulista}; key \"corinthians\" tokens {corinthians} ⊆ ✓ → match corinthians. But also key \"sport\" tokens {sport} ⊆ ✓ → match sport! AMBIGUOUS — two matches: {corinthians, sport} → pick by... hmm. Both are valid interpretations. Frequency pick → corinthians (more matches). Report alternative \"sport\". Acceptable!\n   Also substring fallback: query bare substring of key bare or vice versa (\"palmeiras\" ⊆ \"palmeiras-sp\" — but that's the multi-uf case handled; \"gremio\" vs \"gremio novorizontino\"? query \"gremio\" exact-matches key \"gremio\" first ✓). Only do token-subset when no exact match.\n\n6. Return ResolveResult {key, display, alternatives: [{key, display}], matched_by}.\n\ndisplay(key): most frequent display variant for that key, preferring variants WITHOUT \"-\" suffix (no uf) and without noise. Compute at finalize.\n\nAlso `all_teams()` → list of {key, display, match_count} for list_teams tool.\n\nparse_date: \n```python\ndef parse_date(value):\n    if not value: return None\n    s = str(value).strip()\n    if s.upper() in {\"NA\",\"N/A\",\"-\",\"--\",\"\"}: return None\n    for fmt in (\"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\",\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S\",\"%Y-%m-%d\",\"%d/%m/%Y\",\"%d/%m/%Y %H:%M\"):\n        try: return datetime.strptime(s, fmt).date()\n        except ValueError: continue\n    m = re.match(r\"^(\\d{4})-(\\d{2})-(\\d{2})\", s)  # fallback prefix\n    ...\n    return None\n```\nAlso parse_time for brf \"20:00:00\" — keep as string.\n\nparse_int: handle \"\", \"-\", \"NA\", \"1.0\" → int(float(x)).\n\nNow models.py:\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass Match:\n    date: date | None\n    time: str | None\n    home: str\n    away: str\n    home_key: str\n    away_key: str\n    home_goals: int | None\n    away_goals: int | None\n    competition: str\n    competition_key: str\n    season: int | None\n    round_label: str | None\n    stage: str | None\n    venue: str | None\n    source: str\n    home_corners: int | None = None\n    ... etc\n    def result_valid(self) -> bool\n    def to_dict(self)\n    def summary(self) -> str  # \"2023-09-24: Flamengo 2-1 Fluminense (Brasileirão Série A, Round 22)\"\n```\n\nPlayer dataclass with to_dict().\n\nloaders.py — raw reading:\n```python\ndef load_matches(data_dir) -> list[RawMatch]  # or emit observations\n```\nDesign: loaders produce list of dicts with normalized-ish fields + team display/uf hints; then service builds registry from observations, then finalizes matches. Let me structure:\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass RawMatch:\n    competition_key: str\n    competition: str\n    source: str\n    date: date | None\n    time: str | None\n    home_display: str\n    away_display: str\n    home_uf: str | None\n    away_uf: str | None\n    home_goals: int | None\n    away_goals: int | None\n    season: int | None\n    round_label: str | None\n    stage: str | None\n    venue: str | None\n    stats: dict | None\n```\n\nLoader functions: `_load_brasileirao`, `_load_brazilian_cup`, `_load_libertadores`, `_load_br_football`, `_load_novo`, `_load_fifa_players`. `load_all(data_dir)` returns (raw_matches, players).\n\nCompetition mapping:\n- Brasileirao_Matches.csv → comp_key \"brasileirao-serie-a\", display \"Brasileirão Série A\", source \"brasileirao_matches\" — hmm source names: use readable: \"Brasileirão Matches (Kaggle: jogos-do-campeonato-brasileiro)\"? Keep source ids short: \"brasileirao_matches\", \"copa_do_brasil_matches\", \"libertadores_matches\", \"br_football_stats\", \"campeonato_2003_2019\", \"fifa_data\". And a SOURCE_LABELS dict for display.\n- Brazilian_Cup → \"copa-do-brasil\" / \"Copa do Brasil\".\n- Libertadores → \"copa-libertadores\" / \"Copa Libertadores\".\n- brf: tournament \"Serie A\"→brasileirao-serie-a, \"Serie B\"→brasileirao-serie-b, \"Serie C\"→brasileirao-serie-c, \"Copa do Brasil\"→copa-do-brasil.\n- novo → brasileirao-serie-a.\n\nRound labels: bra round int → str(int); cup round int → f\"Round {n}\"... and stage semantics for final detection handled in service (stage param \"final\" → for copa-do-brasil: max round per season with ≤2 matches; for libertadores: stage == \"final\"). novo Rodada → int. Libertadores stage pass-through (\"group stage\", \"round of 16\", \"quarterfinals\", \"semifinals\", \"final\").\n\nservice.py — SoccerDataService:\n\n```python\nSOURCE_PRIORITY = {\n  (\"brasileirao-serie-a\", range(2012,2023)): [\"brasileirao_matches\"],\n  ...\n}\n```\nImplement as function:\n```python\ndef _sources_for(comp_key, season):\n    if comp_key == \"brasileirao-serie-a\":\n        if season is None: return None  # meaning: resolve per season\n        if 2012 <= season <= 2022: return {\"brasileirao_matches\"}\n        if 2003 <= season <= 2011: return {\"campeonato_2003_2019\"}\n        if season >= 2023: return {\"br_football_stats\"}\n    elif comp_key == \"copa-do-brasil\":\n        if season is None: return None\n        if 2012 <= season <= 2021: return {\"copa_do_brasil_matches\"}\n        return {\"br_football_stats\"}\n    elif comp_key in (\"brasileirao-serie-b\",\"brasileirao-serie-c\"): return {\"br_football_stats\"}\n    elif comp_key == \"copa-libertadores\": return {\"libertadores_matches\"}\n    return None\n```\nPool builder: for comp+season → if sources known, filter matches by (comp, season, source in sources); if season None → group matches by season, resolve per season, union. If comp None → for each (comp, season) pair resolve sources → union. Cache pools? 24k matches scans are fast (<10ms each); no caching needed. Maybe precompute per-comp-season lists dict for speed — yes: `self._by_comp_season = defaultdict(list)` at load; pool = concat of resolved per (comp, season). Fast.\n\nActually — important: also index matches by team key: `self._by_team = defaultdict(list)` mapping key → matches (only pool-resolved? no — team index built AFTER source resolution per (comp,season)... source resolution depends on comp+season, not team. So build team index over the RESOLVED global pool (the pool where each (comp,season) uses preferred source). search_matches uses resolved pool unless source param given (then raw filter). Simplest: keep `self.matches` = ALL raw loaded; `self._resolved_pool` = deduped-by-source-priority list; team index over resolved pool. When user passes source explicitly → filter self.matches.\n\nWait, but resolved pool needs comp+season level: build `self._pool_by_comp_season: dict[(comp, season), list[Match]]` from resolved matches; and `self._resolved_matches` flat list. For search with comp+season+source=None → use per-(comp,season) resolution even without explicit filters: iterate ALL (comp,season) buckets (each already source-resolved). ✓.\n\nFor source-explicit queries → use `self._raw_by_source[(source, comp, season)]`? Just filter self.matches in place (linear scan of 24k — fine).\n\nMethods detail:\n\n**search_matches(team, opponent, competition, season, date_from, date_to, stage, venue?, source, limit)**:\n- Resolve team names (resolve_team) — team required? Make team optional; opponent optional (requires team).\n- Competition resolve: `_resolve_competition(competition)` accepts display/key/fuzzy.\n- Get pool (resolved or source-filtered).\n- Filter: team involved (home_key or away_key == team.key); if opponent: other side == opponent.key (either order). comp, season, dates, stage.\n- stage \"final\": comp-specific logic (copa-do-brasil max-round≤2; libertadores stage==\"final\"; others: round_label lowercase contains \"final\").\n- Sort by date (None dates last), ascending. limit (default 30). Return dict: {\"matches\": [m.to_dict() ...], \"total\": N, \"truncated\": bool, \"team\": display, ...}.\n\n**head_to_head(team, opponent, competition=None, season=None)**: filter pool both teams; separate wins: for each match determine winner from perspective; aggregate {team_a_wins, team_b_wins, draws, goals_a, goals_b}; matches list (all, or capped 50). Return summary + matches.\n\n**team_stats(team, season=None, competition=None, venue=None)**: pool filter; compute overall + per-competition + home/away splits: matches, wins, draws, losses, gf, ga, win_rate. venue filter: \"home\"/\"away\"/None.\n\n**team_competitions(team)**: from resolved pool: per comp: matches count + seasons list.\n\n**standings(competition=\"Brasileirão Série A\", season=required, source=None)**: league comps only (brasileirao-serie-a/b/c); pool filter; if len(matches with results)==0 → error. Compute table; return rows with position, team, display, played, w/d/l, gf, ga, gd, pts, plus champion=first, relegated=last4 (if ≥ 18 teams? relegated = bottom 4 for Serie A 20-team seasons; generic: bottom 20% round? I'll do bottom 4 if ≥16 teams else bottom 2). Also note \"matches_considered\" count.\n\n**competition_info(competition=None)**: list competitions with: display, key, seasons (min-max + list), match counts (resolved), sources used. If competition given → detail + per-season match counts.\n\n**derbies(season=None, competition=None)**: iterate pool; match unordered pair in DERBIES; group by derby name; return per derby: name, teams, match count, first/last date, sample recent matches (5), h2h summary. If season given → filter.\n\n**biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, n=10)**: pool with valid results; margin = |hg-ag|; sort by margin desc, then goals desc; return top n.\n\n**league_statistics(competition=None, season=None)**: pool valid results: total, avg_goals_per_match, home_wins, draws, away_wins, home_win_rate, avg_home_goals, avg_away_goals, biggest_win ref. If competition None → per-competition breakdown + overall? Keep: overall across pool + maybe per comp if no comp given. I'll return overall stats + per_competition list when comp not specified.\n\n**best_records(competition=None, season=None, venue=\"home\", min_matches=10)**: per team aggregate at venue; win rate + points; sort by win rate desc then wins; top 10. Also support venue=\"away\",\"overall\".\n\n**search_players(name=None, club=None, nationality=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, limit=30, sort=\"overall\")**: filters; name substring accent-insens; club substring display OR club_key match; position exact (or substring? \"forward\"→? FIFA positions are ST, LW... map group names: \"forward\"→[ST,CF,LW,RW,LW...]? I'll implement position group mapping: goalkeeper→GK; defender→[CB,LB,RB,LWB,RWB]; midfielder→[CDM,CM,CAM,LM,RM]; forward/attacker→[ST,CF,LW,RW]. And exact codes pass through.) sort by overall desc default; limit.\n\n**top_players(club=None, nationality=None, position=None, n=10)** → top by overall.\n\n**players_by_club(nationality=\"Brazil\")**: Brazilian players (or given nat) at Brazilian clubs (club_key in registry AND club_key corresponds to a team with matches? I'll define Brazilian clubs = club_key that exists in team registry — but registry includes foreign Libertadores clubs! E.g. FIFA \"Boca Juniors\"? is Boca in fifa? maybe. So filter: club_key in registry AND registry says team played in Brazilian competitions (brasileirao/copa-do-brasil)? Simpler: club_key in set of teams from brasileirao/copa comps. I'll compute `self._brazilian_team_keys` from comps brasileirao-* ∪ copa-do-brasil (domestic). Return per club: club display, player count, avg overall, top player names (3).\n\n**resolve_team(name)** → dict {key, display, alternatives, matched_by} or {error, suggestions}.\n\n**list_teams(query=None, competition=None, season=None)**: registry teams filtered by substring; with match counts; if competition/season → only teams in that pool.\n\nserver.py with FastMCP:\n\n```python\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nmcp = FastMCP(\"brazilian-soccer\")\n_service = SoccerDataService()  # lazy singleton\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef search_matches(team: str | None = None, ...) -> dict: ...\n...\ndef main(): mcp.run()\n```\n\nLazy service: module-level `_svc = None; def _service(): global _svc; ...` — load on first tool call so import stays fast (and tests control loading). Actually for stdio server, load at startup is fine; but lazy keeps tests snappy. Use lazy.\n\nTests:\n\nconftest.py:\n```python\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"session\")\ndef service(): return SoccerDataService(DATA_DIR)\n```\nDATA_DIR = repo_root/data/kaggle — compute from __file__ parents.\n\nFeature files (Gherkin) + step defs. pytest-bdd 8.1: scenarios(\"features/match_queries.feature\"), @given/@when/@then decorators with parse. Session-scoped service + pytest-bdd: step functions can use fixtures. Note: pytest-bdd + session-scoped fixture is fine.\n\nFeature files:\n1. features/match_queries.feature — scenarios: find matches between two teams; matches by team+season; find cup finals; matches by date range.\n2. features/team_queries.feature — team stats home record; head-to-head compare; team competitions.\n3. features/player_queries.feature — search player by name; Brazilian players top; players at club; forwards from club.\n4. features/competition_queries.feature — standings 2019 champion; relegated teams; competition info seasons.\n5. features/statistics_queries.feature — avg goals; biggest wins; best home record; derbies.\n\nSteps implemented against the service (unit-level BDD), plus a separate MCP protocol test (test_mcp_server.py) using in-memory client session to verify tools/list + a tools/call round trip — that validates \"MCP server\" integration truly.\n\nLet me check the mcp SDK in-memory test API quickly (mcp 2.1.1). I recall:\n```python\nfrom mcp.shared.memory import create_client_server_memory_streams\nfrom mcp import ClientSession\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nasync def test():\n    app = FastMCP(\"t\")\n    @app.tool()...\n    async with create_client_server_memory_streams() as (client_streams, server_streams):\n        async with ClientSession(*client_streams) as client:\n            server = app._mcp_server\n            async with server.run(server_streams[0], server_streams[1], app._mcp_server.create_initialization_options()):\n                tools = await client.list_tools()\n                res = await client.call_tool(\"search_matches\", {...})\n```\nI'll verify the exact API from the installed package source before writing.\n\nGherkin scenario example:\n```gherkin\nFeature: Match Queries\n  Scenario: Find matches between two teams\n    Given the match data is loaded\n    When I search for matches between \"Flamengo\" and \"Fluminense\"\n    Then I should receive a list of matches\n    And each match should have date, scores, and competition\n```\n\nStep defs:\n```python\nfrom pytest_bdd import scenarios, given, when, then, parsers\nscenarios(\"../features/match_queries.feature\")\n\n@given(\"the match data is loaded\", target_fixture=\"svc\")\ndef svc(service): return service\n\n@when(parsers.parse('I search for matches between \"{a}\" and \"{b}\"'), target_fixture=\"result\")\ndef search(svc, a, b): return svc.search_matches(team=a, opponent=b)\n\n@then(\"I should receive a list of matches\")\ndef check_list(result): assert result[\"total\"] > 0; assert result[\"matches\"]\n\n@then(\"each match should have date, scores, and competition\")\ndef check_fields(result): ...\n```\n\nSome expected values to assert (verified from data):\n- Fla-Flu h2h across all comps (resolved pool): count TBD — compute in test setup? BDD asserts should be meaningful: assert total >= 20, all matches involve both teams.\n- Palmeiras 2023 matches: from brf (2023) — Palmeiras 2023 Serie A ~38 + libertadores 2023? lib has 2022 max... lib seasons 2013-2022 (+NA). So Palmeiras 2023 = Serie A (brf) + Copa do Brasil 2023 (brf) ~ 50ish. Assert >= 40.\n- Cup finals: stage=\"final\" → 2012-2020 finals, 2 matches per season two-legged: expect ~16 matches, each season's final teams.\n- Standings 2019: Flamengo champion 90 pts, 28W 6D 4L (matches spec exactly!). Santos 74 pts (2nd on tiebreak? Santos 22W=74, Palmeiras 21W11D=74 — spec example shows Santos 2nd, Palmeiras 3rd → my tiebreak: pts, then WINS (22>21) → Santos 2nd ✓ matches spec AND reality).\n- Relegated 2019: bottom 4 = Avaí(20), CSA(32), Chapecoense(32), Cruzeiro(36). Real relegations 2019: Avaí, CSA, Chapecoense, Cruzeiro ✓.\n- Corinthians home record 2022 (spec example: 2022 Brasileirão): compute from data; assert structure + plausible (matches=19, wins>0 etc). Spec example says 2022: 19 matches — assert matches==19, W+D+L==19. (Real 2022 Corinthians home: 19 matches... 2022 had 19 home games ✓ assert 19.)\n- Gabriel Barbosa in FIFA data ✓ (check exists).\n- Neymar top Brazilian: overall 92 (spec example says Neymar 92 ✓ verified in data exploration above — sample printed \"Neymar Jr Paris Saint-Germain 92\").\n- Avg goals Brasileirão ~2.4-2.6.\n- Biggest win: margin ≥ 6.\n- Gre-Nal derby matches exist.\n- \"When did Flamengo last play Corinthians\" → last match date < 2023-01-01.\n\nAlso unit tests: normalize (names variations: \"Palmeiras-SP\" vs \"Palmeiras\" vs \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\"; dates \"29/03/2003\", \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\"; accent \"Grêmio\"→\"gremio\"), loaders (all 6 files, counts within tolerance: bras 4180, cup 1337, lib 1255, brf 10296, novo 6886, fifa 18207).\n\nTime to write everything. Let me check the mcp SDK API for the in-memory test first.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0593c1e6a0016tOosgzFVGDCQB","sessionID":"ses_fa6cfe050ffe51wxe0DFh4LAA3","messageID":"msg_05939f28a001YDI1M27eQN714u"}}
