{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788204199208,"sessionID":"ses_fa6cfe050ffe51wxe0DFh4LAA3","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_2643fe983fe746e4a9584a52","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"Feature: Match Queries\n  As a soccer fan asking questions in natural language\n  I want to search the match datasets by team, date, competition and season\n  So that I can find fixtures, scores and competitions\n\n  Scenario: Find matches between two teams\n    Given the match data is loaded\n    When I search for matches between \"Flamengo\" and \"Fluminense\"\n    Then I should receive a list of matches\n    And each match should have date, scores, and competition\n\n  Scenario: Get team statistics\n    Given the match data is loaded\n    When I request statistics for \"Palmeiras\" in season 2023\n    Then I should receive wins, losses, draws, and goals\n\n  Scenario: Find matches for a team in a season\n    Given the match data is loaded\n    When I search for matches of team \"Palmeiras\" in season 2023\n    Then I should receive a list of matches\n    And every match should involve the team \"Palmeiras\"\n    And every match should be from season 2023\n\n  Scenario: Find all Copa do Brasil finals\n    Given the match data is loaded\n    When I search for finals of competition \"Copa do Brasil\"\n    Then every match should be from competition \"Copa do Brasil\"\n    And each season should contribute at most 2 finals\n    And the dataset should contain 18 finals\n\n  Scenario: Find matches by date range\n    Given the match data is loaded\n    When I search for matches from \"2019-11-01\" to \"2019-11-30\" in competition \"Brasileirão Série A\" and season 2019\n    Then every match should fall within the date range\n    And the result should not be empty\n\n  Scenario: Find the most recent match between two teams\n    Given the match data is loaded\n    When I search for matches between \"Flamengo\" and \"Corinthians\"\n    Then the last match should be on \"2023-10-08\"\n    And the last match should have a score\n\n  Scenario: Reject unknown teams politely\n    Given the match data is loaded\n    When I search for matches of team \"Real Madrid\"\n    Then the response should explain the team was not found\n","filePath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-e6k_mc30/retort-9f7589d26ec0/tests/features/match_queries.feature"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-e6k_mc30/retort-9f7589d26ec0/tests/features/match_queries.feature","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"tests/features/match_queries.feature","time":{"start":1788204199205,"end":1788204199207}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Standings match reality for completed seasons:\n- 2018: Palmeiras champion ✓, relegated Sport/América-MG/Vitória/Paraná ✓ (real!)\n- 2020: Flamengo ✓, relegated Vasco/Goiás/Coritiba/Botafogo ✓ (real!)\n- 2021: Atlético-MG ✓, relegated Grêmio/Bahia/Sport/Chapecoense ✓ (real!)\n- 2023: Grêmio champion with 68 pts — real 2023 champion was Palmeiras with 82... but the brf 2023 dataset only has 377 matches and apparently incomplete/skewed (real Grêmio was 2nd with 68 points!). Grêmio actually finished 2nd with 68 pts in 2023 ✓ — so the dataset is missing Palmeiras-heavy matches (377/380). The 2023 table is only approximately right — it's the data we have. Tests should avoid asserting 2023 champion; instead assert structure and most-goals (Grêmio 63 — real Grêmio scored 69; data says 63, fine — assert from computed table).\n\nAlso \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\" display — hmm, display for vasco key picked brf's \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\" over bra's \"Vasco da Gama-RJ\"? Minor cosmetic. Meh.\n\nNow write all tests. Also add a `pytest.ini`/`pyproject.toml` for pytest config (testpaths, markers, bdd features base dir). requirements.txt.\n\nOne consideration: pytest-bdd `scenarios()` needs feature files relative to the test file. Also pytest-asyncio? Not needed — I'll use anyio.run() inside a sync test to avoid extra deps. Actually anyio is a dependency of mcp ✓ available.\n\nconftest.py:\n```python\nimport pytest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom brazilian_soccer_mcp.service import SoccerDataService\n\nDATA_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1] / \"data\" / \"kaggle\"\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"session\")\ndef service():\n    return SoccerDataService(DATA_DIR)\n```\n\nFor BDD steps, each test module does `scenarios(\"features/match_queries.feature\")` — path relative to the test file location ✓.\n\nNow, the feature files + step definitions. Let me carefully map the steps I'll implement. Shared steps across modules (given \"the match data is loaded\" / \"the player data is loaded\") — define per module (pytest-bdd finds steps in the same module).\n\nLet me write everything now. First feature files.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05946ad22001Q3UeuiDagib7Ls","sessionID":"ses_fa6cfe050ffe51wxe0DFh4LAA3","messageID":"msg_059465cdd00120uVz8cdftH43g"}}
