{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788204553937,"sessionID":"ses_fa6cfe050ffe51wxe0DFh4LAA3","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_424559d8782a41b3afaff5ba","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"./venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nfrom brazilian_soccer_mcp.service import SoccerDataService\nsvc = SoccerDataService(\"data/kaggle\")\nreg = svc.registry\nprint(\"'vasco da gama' in displays:\", \"vasco da gama\" in reg._displays)\nprint(\"'vasco da gama' in key_counts:\", \"vasco da gama\" in reg._key_counts, reg._key_counts.get(\"vasco da gama\"))\nprint(\"'vasco' in key_counts:\", reg._key_counts.get(\"vasco\"))\nprint(\"displays for vasco da gama:\", reg._displays.get(\"vasco da gama\"))\nprint(\"displays for vasco:\", reg._displays.get(\"vasco\"))\n# what keys do matches use?\nfrom collections import Counter\nc = Counter()\nfor m in svc.matches:\n    for k in (m.home_key, m.away_key):\n        if \"vasco\" in k: c[k]+=1\nprint(\"match keys:\", dict(c))\nprint(\"resolve Vasco:\", reg.resolve(\"Vasco\").key)\nprint(\"canonical Vasco:\", reg.canonical(\"Vasco\"))\nprint(\"canonical 'Vasco da Gama-RJ':\", reg.canonical(\"Vasco da Gama-RJ\"))\nEOF"},"output":"'vasco da gama' in displays: False\n'vasco da gama' in key_counts: False None\n'vasco' in key_counts: 558\ndisplays for vasco da gama: None\ndisplays for vasco: Vasco\nmatch keys: {'vasco gama': 739, 'vasco da gama': 558}\nresolve Vasco: vasco\ncanonical Vasco: vasco da gama\ncanonical 'Vasco da Gama-RJ': vasco gama\n","metadata":{"output":"'vasco da gama' in displays: False\n'vasco da gama' in key_counts: False None\n'vasco' in key_counts: 558\ndisplays for vasco da gama: None\ndisplays for vasco: Vasco\nmatch keys: {'vasco gama': 739, 'vasco da gama': 558}\nresolve Vasco: vasco\ncanonical Vasco: vasco da gama\ncanonical 'Vasco da Gama-RJ': vasco gama\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"./venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nfrom brazilian_soccer_mcp.service import SoccerDataService\nsvc = SoccerDataService(\"data/kaggle\")\nreg = svc.registry\nprint(\"'vasco da gama' in displays:\", \"vasco da gama\" in reg._displays)\nprint(\"'vasco da gama' in key_counts:\", \"vasco da gama\" in reg._key_counts, reg._key_counts.get(\"vasco da gama\"))\nprint(\"'vasco' in key_counts:\", reg._key_counts.get(\"vasco\"))\nprint(\"displays for vasco da gama:\", reg._displays.get(\"vasco da gama\"))\nprint(\"displays for vasco:\", reg._displays.get(\"vasco\"))\n# what keys do matches use?\nfrom collections import Counter\nc = Counter()\nfor m in svc.matches:\n    for k in (m.home_key, m.away_key):\n        if \"vasco\" in k: c[k]+=1\nprint(\"match keys:\", dict(c))\nprint(\"resolve Vasco:\", reg.resolve(\"Vasco\").key)\nprint(\"canonical Vasco:\", reg.canonical(\"Vasco\"))\nprint(\"canonical 'Vasco da Gama-RJ':\", reg.canonical(\"Vasco da Gama-RJ\"))\nEOF","time":{"start":1788204553918,"end":1788204553936}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The lowercase \"vasco da gama\" display doesn't come from raw data — all raw variants are capitalized. So display(\"vasco da gama\") returning lowercase \"vasco da gama\" means... the key itself is being used as display because `_displays` doesn't have the key! Why? The key for Vasco: novo \"Vasco\" bare + UF RJ hint → state (\"vasco\", RJ, None) → key \"vasco-rj\"?? WAIT — bare \"vasco\" ≠ \"vasco da gama\". novo stores team as \"Vasco\" (not \"Vasco da Gama\")! And lib \"Vasco\" too. So:\n- state (\"vasco\", RJ, None) from novo → bare \"vasco\" has ufs {RJ} only → key \"vasco\"\n- state (\"vasco da gama\", RJ, None) from bra \"Vasco da Gama-RJ\" → key... bare \"vasco da gama\" ufs {RJ} only → key \"vasco da gama\"\n- brf \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\" → state (\"vasco da gama\", RJ) → key \"vasco da gama\" ✓\n- cup \"Vasco da Gama - RJ\" → (\"vasco da gama\", RJ) ✓\n\nSo novo/lib \"Vasco\" → key \"vasco\", while bra/brf/cup → key \"vasco da gama\"! TWO KEYS for the same club!! The alias \"vasco\"→\"vasco da gama\" is in ALIASES — canonical(\"Vasco\") checks alias: parsed.bare \"vasco\" → alias \"vasco\" → \"vasco da gama\" ✓!! So canonical() DOES map novo \"Vasco\" → \"vasco da gama\". But the REGISTRY finalize computed key \"vasco\" for state (\"vasco\", RJ) and displays recorded under... wait — in the NEW registry design, observe() doesn't assign keys; displays are stored per STATE. At finalize, state (\"vasco\", RJ, None) gets key \"vasco\" (bare single uf) — hmm, but wait: `_key_of_state[(\"vasco\", RJ, None)] = \"vasco\"`. But canonical() checks ALIASES FIRST → returns \"vasco da gama\" for parsed bare \"vasco\". So the state's computed key \"vasco\" is only used... by `_candidates_for_bare` and resolve() — resolve(\"Vasco\") checks alias first ✓ → \"vasco da gama\".\n\nProblem: the displays recorded under state (\"vasco\", RJ, None) — namely \"Vasco\" ×552 from novo — get keyed to \"vasco\" in `_displays[\"vasco\"]` and `_key_counts[\"vasco\"]`. And `_key_counts[\"vasco da gama\"]` gets bra+brf+cup counts. Two entries in the registry: \"vasco\" (552) and \"vasco da gama\" (739). When players_by_club / best_records / standings iterate matches, the match keys use canonical() (alias-aware) → all \"vasco da gama\" ✓ consistent. But list_teams shows both \"vasco\" (display \"Vasco\") and \"vasco da gama\"... and registry.display(\"vasco da gama\"): displays from states that map to key \"vasco da gama\" = bra \"Vasco da Gama-RJ\" (266), cup \"Vasco da Gama - RJ\" (54), brf \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\" (419). Simple check: \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\" (no hyphen) → simple → wins. So display \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\"... but the smoke test showed display(\"vasco da gama\") = \"vasco da gama\" LOWERCASE?! That's neither of those three. Unless... `self._displays.setdefault(key, key)` in finalize — for keys in _key_counts without displays? No — \"vasco da gama\" HAS displays.\n\nOH WAIT. I see — the smoke test called `svc.registry.display(\"vasco da gama\")` and got \"vasco da gama\". Hmm. Let me look at the display selection again... displays dict for key \"vasco da gama\": {\"Vasco da Gama-RJ\": 266, \"Vasco da Gama - RJ\": 54, \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\": 419}. score: simple=(no \"-\" and no \"(\"): \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\" → 1 → wins. Result should be \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\". But I got lowercase...\n\nHmm — did my edit to _pick_display actually apply? Let me check the current file... Or maybe the smoke test output \"vasco da gama\" came from `display()` falling through `self._displays.get(key, key)` → key not in _displays → returns the key itself (lowercase)! So \"vasco da gama\" is NOT in _displays! Why? ... OH. I see the bug! In finalize():\n\n```python\nkey_counts: dict[str, int] = {}\nkey_displays: dict[str, dict[str, int]] = {}\nfor state, count in self._state_counts.items():\n    key = self._key_of_state[state]\n    ...\nfor key in set(ALIASES.values()):\n    key_counts.setdefault(key, 0)\nself._key_counts = {k: v for k, v in key_counts.items() if v > 0}\nself._displays = {key: self._pick_display(displays) for key, displays in key_displays.items()}\n```\n\n`_key_counts` for \"vasco da gama\": from states (\"vasco da gama\", RJ, None) → 266+419+54=739 ✓ in key_counts. `key_displays[\"vasco da gama\"]` = those displays ✓ → _displays should have it...\n\nHmm wait, unless the score picks differently. Actually let me just debug directly instead of guessing.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0594c1353001P7Or0dIGZNYJqm","sessionID":"ses_fa6cfe050ffe51wxe0DFh4LAA3","messageID":"msg_0594bbd90001WQQ5gBK9P9iBj3"}}
